200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (Lights Still On was created to highlight the experiences of domestic violence agencies across North Carolina and to examine how ongoing funding challenges have affected both advocates and the services available to survivors. Through agency perspectives, workforce experiences, and an analysis of trends related to federal funding reductions, the lasting impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic, rising operational costs, and increasing service demands, the film explores the factors contributing to advocate burnout, workforce turnover, and resource limitations within the domestic violence field.)
/E (Lights Still On was created to highlight the experiences of domestic violence agencies across North Carolina and to examine how ongoing funding challenges have affected both advocates and the services available to survivors. Through agency perspectives, workforce experiences, and an analysis of trends related to federal funding reductions, the lasting impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic, rising operational costs, and increasing service demands, the film explores the factors contributing to advocate burnout, workforce turnover, and resource limitations within the domestic violence field.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (The film seeks to elevate the voices of those working on the front lines while drawing attention to the critical role that sustainable funding plays in ensuring the safety of survivors and their access to services. It demonstrates that domestic violence programs are more than organizations, they are lifelines that provide crisis intervention, emergency shelter, legal advocacy, counseling, prevention education, and pathways to healing.)
/E (The film seeks to elevate the voices of those working on the front lines while drawing attention to the critical role that sustainable funding plays in ensuring the safety of survivors and their access to services. It demonstrates that domestic violence programs are more than organizations, they are lifelines that provide crisis intervention, emergency shelter, legal advocacy, counseling, prevention education, and pathways to healing.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (Lights Still On reminds us that healing, safety, and hope do not happen alone. Behind every survivor served is an advocate, a crisis counselor, a shelter bed, a case manager, and a community committed to keeping the lights on. As domestic violence programs face increasing financial challenges, sustaining these resources is not only an investment in services, it is an investment in safety, dignity, and the possibility of a future free from violence.)
/E (Lights Still On reminds us that healing, safety, and hope do not happen alone. Behind every survivor served is an advocate, a crisis counselor, a shelter bed, a case manager, and a community committed to keeping the lights on. As domestic violence programs face increasing financial challenges, sustaining these resources is not only an investment in services, it is an investment in safety, dignity, and the possibility of a future free from violence.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
