/K [5]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (The demand for domestic violence services continues to exceed available resources across North Carolina. Service requests increased by 75% between 2018 and 2021 and remain approximately 50% higher than pre-pandemic levels. In 2023 alone, more than 6,000 survivors seeking shelter were turned away because agencies lacked the capacity to meet demand. At the same time, Victims of Crime Act \(VOCA\) funding has declined by 76%, dropping from $103 million in 2018 to $24.6 million in 2024. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 166 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [610 0 R 0]
/K [7]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (As agencies struggle to meet growing needs and the challenges of COVID-19, domestic violence-related homicides continue to rise. North Carolina recorded 93 domestic violence homicides in 2024, nearly 70% more than six years earlier. In the first three months of 2025, 19 people were killed, with half of those deaths involving intimate partner murder-suicides. Firearms were involved in nearly all of these fatalities, highlighting the increasingly lethal nature of domestic violence and the critical need for sustained investment in survivor services and prevention efforts.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
